description:
## What

AGENTS.md non-negotiable #6 requires that result/error types be `Send + Sync`. Almost certainly true today because no current load-bearing type contains `Rc<T>`, `RefCell<T>`, or raw pointers — Send+Sync auto-derives for owned-data types. But the rule is currently aspirational; a one-shot audit makes it compile-enforced.

## Targets

- pkix-path: ValidatedPath, Error, TrustAnchor, ValidationPolicy
- pkix-chain: Error
- pkix-revocation: Error
- pkix-truststore: Error, TrustAnchor (re-exported)
- pkix-lint: Finding
- pkix-identity: types as they land

## Implementation

For each target, add a compile-time assertion. Easiest pattern is the `static_assertions` crate (or a hand-rolled `fn _assert_send_sync<T: Send + Sync>() {}` + `_assert_send_sync::<ValidatedPath>();` at module scope).

Example:

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
fn _assert_traits() {
    fn _send_sync<T: Send + Sync>() {}
    _send_sync::<ValidatedPath>();
    _send_sync::<Error>();
    _send_sync::<TrustAnchor>();
    _send_sync::<ValidationPolicy>();
}
```

Or under the unit test module:

```rust
const _: fn() = || {
    fn _assert<T: Send + Sync>() {}
    _assert::<ValidatedPath>();
    // ...
};
```

## Acceptance

- [ ] Compile-time Send+Sync assertions on every load-bearing result type
- [ ] cargo check / cargo test succeeds across workspace
- [ ] If any type fails the assertion, fix is in scope (likely Box / Arc / replace Rc with Arc, etc.)
- [ ] CHANGELOG entries if any types had to change

## Out of scope

- Adding Send+Sync to types that aren't user-facing result types (internal scratch state, builder intermediates)
- Documenting Send+Sync in rustdoc (rustdoc auto-shows trait impls)

## Why

AGENTS.md non-negotiable #6 requires Send+Sync as a MUST. Compile-time assertions prevent regression as new fields land (especially in Error enums where someone could add a variant carrying Rc<T> by accident).
